// Compiled by bin/build-help.php from help/*.md (the add-on's topics only). Do not edit.
export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"partcraft","addon":"open-solvers","name":"Open solver cases","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"open-solvers-start","scope":"addon:open-solvers","title":"Open solver cases: set up here, run in an outside solver","summary":"Add the Open solver cases add-on, pick faces, export a ready-to-run case folder for OpenFOAM, code_aster or OpenRadioss, run it on your own computer, and bring the results back onto the model.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["open solver cases","openfoam","code_aster","openradioss","cfd","flow","snappyhexmesh","case folder","export","zip","vtk","drop test","crash","explicit","modal","thermal","add-on","simulation"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"the-steps-for-every-kind","text":"The steps, for every kind","level":2},{"id":"a-flow-case","text":"A flow case","level":2},{"id":"a-stress-frequency-or-heat-study","text":"A stress, frequency or heat study","level":2},{"id":"a-drop-or-impact-run","text":"A drop or impact run","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen solver cases is a PartCraft add-on. It comes with PartCraft, is free, and is off until you add it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen solver cases\u003C/span\u003E. Its tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolver cases\u003C/span\u003E, is then in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt does three things: you set a study up on the part by picking faces, it writes a complete case folder as a .zip for an outside solver, and it reads the solver&#039;s result files back and draws them on the model. Three kinds of case:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlow (for OpenFOAM)\u003C/span\u003E: air or water around the model, or through it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStress, frequencies or heat (for code_aster)\u003C/span\u003E: linear static, natural frequencies, steady heat.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrop or impact (for OpenRadioss)\u003C/span\u003E: a drop onto a rigid floor, an impact, faces pushed at a speed. PartCraft does not solve this kind itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe solvers are separate programs. They are not part of CadCraft and nothing of them is included: you add them to your own computer yourself, from each project&#039;s own page (the panel lists them under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat you need on your computer\u003C/span\u003E, with each licence). CadCraft cannot start a program on your computer, so you run the solver yourself with the commands in the README of each case.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo case from this add-on has yet been run in the real solvers. The files are written to each project&#039;s published formats and checked for structure and units, but check a case before you rely on it. An analysis aid, not a design approval; your engineer reviews the results.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-steps-for-every-kind\"\u003EThe steps, for every kind\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the part and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolver cases\u003C/span\u003E tab. Choose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind of case\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaces\u003C/span\u003E, press an Add button (for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd fixed faces\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd inlet\u003C/span\u003E), then click the faces in the 3D view. The face under the pointer lights up; click again to take one off. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone picking\u003C/span\u003E. Type the value that goes with it. The small × removes an item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport case (.zip)\u003C/span\u003E. The panel lists the files it wrote. If something is missing (no inlet, nothing holds the part) it says so and writes nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnzip the folder on the computer that has the solver and follow its README: it gives the exact commands and the units.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBack in PartCraft press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport results\u003C/span\u003E, choose the result files, choose what to plot, and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow on the model\u003C/span\u003E. Blue is the lowest value, red the highest.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change is one undo step and the settings are saved with the part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-flow-case\"\u003EA flow case\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose which OpenFOAM the case is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWritten for\u003C/span\u003E: the two lines of the program use a few different file names and keywords, and each file says which version it was matched to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAround the model (wind tunnel)\u003C/span\u003E: the model is a body in a box of fluid. Set the speed, the flow direction and how far the box reaches upstream, downstream and to the sides (in model sizes). The box faces are the inlet, the outlet and slip sides. You need not pick anything; a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENamed wall\u003C/span\u003E only gives some faces their own patch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInside: the model is the fluid space\u003C/span\u003E: model the fluid itself as a solid (the inside of the duct, not its walls). Add an inlet and an outlet and pick their faces; faces you do not pick are walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat will be written\u003C/span\u003E shows the Reynolds number, the size of the fluid box, the number of background cells, the smallest cell, the point in the fluid the mesher needs (found and checked by PartCraft) and, for the turbulent model, the inlet values worked out from the intensity I and the length scale L: k = 1.5 (U I)² and ω = √k / (0.09^¼ L).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERun it: unzip, open a terminal where OpenFOAM is loaded, go into the folder, then \u003Ccode\u003Echmod +x Allrun\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E./Allrun\u003C/code\u003E. The README lists the same commands one at a time (features, blockMesh, snappyHexMesh, checkMesh, the solver, foamToVTK). Results: choose the .vtk files of the wall patches from the VTK folder, and the forces text file under postProcessing/forces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-stress-frequency-or-heat-study\"\u003EA stress, frequency or heat study\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the study, the material (for heat, the thermal conductivity) and the faces: fixed faces, forces (a total in newtons spread over the faces), pressures (MPa, positive pushes in), or temperatures, heat fluxes and convection. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy from this part&#039;s study\u003C/span\u003E brings in the fixtures and loads of the part&#039;s Simulation study or Solid FEM settings. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMesh the part\u003C/span\u003E makes the tetrahedral mesh (the export does it if you have not).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERun it: unzip, then \u003Ccode\u003Erun_aster name.export\u003C/code\u003E in the folder (\u003Ccode\u003Eas_run name.export\u003C/code\u003E with version 14 or older). Results: choose the .resu file. Keep the element size as it was when the case was written: the result is matched to the mesh by node number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-drop-or-impact-run\"\u003EA drop or impact run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the elements, the material law (elastic, or Johnson-Cook with your own numbers: none are preset), the starting speed of the whole part, the rigid floor (which side it is on and the gap to it), gravity, self-contact, the run time and the number of result frames. A 1 m fall arrives at about 4.43 m/s.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERun it: unzip, then \u003Ccode\u003Estarter_linux64_gf -i name_0000.rad -np 1\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eengine_linux64_gf -i name_0001.rad\u003C/code\u003E. Turn each animation file into VTK with the solver&#039;s own tool, \u003Ccode\u003Eanim_to_vtk_linux64_gf nameA001 &gt; name_001.vtk\u003C/code\u003E, then choose the .vtk files; several at once gives the frames.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Open solver cases is a PartCraft add-on. It comes with PartCraft, is free, and is off until you add it in Settings › General › Add-ons › Open solver cases. Its tab, Solver cases, is then in the right panel. It does three things: you set a study up on the part by picking faces, it writes a complete case folder as a .zip for an outside solver, and it reads the solver's result files back and draws them on the model. Three kinds of case: Flow (for OpenFOAM): air or water around the model, or through it. Stress, frequencies or heat (for code_aster): linear static, natural frequencies, steady heat. Drop or impact (for OpenRadioss): a drop onto a rigid floor, an impact, faces pushed at a speed. PartCraft does not solve this kind itself. The solvers are separate programs. They are not part of CadCraft and nothing of them is included: you add them to your own computer yourself, from each project's own page (the panel lists them under What you need on your computer, with each licence). CadCraft cannot start a program on your computer, so you run the solver yourself with the commands in the README of each case. No case from this add-on has yet been run in the real solvers. The files are written to each project's published formats and checked for structure and units, but check a case before you rely on it. An analysis aid, not a design approval; your engineer reviews the results. The steps, for every kind Open the part and the Solver cases tab. Choose the Kind of case. Under Faces, press an Add button (for example Add fixed faces or Add inlet), then click the faces in the 3D view. The face under the pointer lights up; click again to take one off. Press Done picking. Type the value that goes with it. The small × removes an item. Press Export case (.zip). The panel lists the files it wrote. If something is missing (no inlet, nothing holds the part) it says so and writes nothing. Unzip the folder on the computer that has the solver and follow its README: it gives the exact commands and the units. Back in PartCraft press Import results, choose the result files, choose what to plot, and press Show on the model. Blue is the lowest value, red the highest. Every change is one undo step and the settings are saved with the part. A flow case Choose which OpenFOAM the case is Written for: the two lines of the program use a few different file names and keywords, and each file says which version it was matched to. Around the model (wind tunnel): the model is a body in a box of fluid. Set the speed, the flow direction and how far the box reaches upstream, downstream and to the sides (in model sizes). The box faces are the inlet, the outlet and slip sides. You need not pick anything; a Named wall only gives some faces their own patch. Inside: the model is the fluid space: model the fluid itself as a solid (the inside of the duct, not its walls). Add an inlet and an outlet and pick their faces; faces you do not pick are walls. What will be written shows the Reynolds number, the size of the fluid box, the number of background cells, the smallest cell, the point in the fluid the mesher needs (found and checked by PartCraft) and, for the turbulent model, the inlet values worked out from the intensity I and the length scale L: k = 1.5 (U I)² and ω = √k / (0.09^¼ L). Run it: unzip, open a terminal where OpenFOAM is loaded, go into the folder, then chmod +x Allrun and ./Allrun. The README lists the same commands one at a time (features, blockMesh, snappyHexMesh, checkMesh, the solver, foamToVTK). Results: choose the .vtk files of the wall patches from the VTK folder, and the forces text file under postProcessing/forces. A stress, frequency or heat study Choose the study, the material (for heat, the thermal conductivity) and the faces: fixed faces, forces (a total in newtons spread over the faces), pressures (MPa, positive pushes in), or temperatures, heat fluxes and convection. Copy from this part's study brings in the fixtures and loads of the part's Simulation study or Solid FEM settings. Mesh the part makes the tetrahedral mesh (the export does it if you have not). Run it: unzip, then run_aster name.export in the folder (as_run name.export with version 14 or older). Results: choose the .resu file. Keep the element size as it was when the case was written: the result is matched to the mesh by node number. A drop or impact run Choose the elements, the material law (elastic, or Johnson-Cook with your own numbers: none are preset), the starting speed of the whole part, the rigid floor (which side it is on and the gap to it), gravity, self-contact, the run time and the number of result frames. A 1 m fall arrives at about 4.43 m/s. Run it: unzip, then starter_linux64_gf -i name_0000.rad -np 1 and engine_linux64_gf -i name_0001.rad. Turn each animation file into VTK with the solver's own tool, anim_to_vtk_linux64_gf nameA001 \u003E name_001.vtk, then choose the .vtk files; several at once gives the frames."},{"slug":"open-solvers-limits","scope":"addon:open-solvers","title":"Open solver cases: units, what was checked and what was not","summary":"The unit set of each kind of case, what the written files were checked against, what has never been run, and what the add-on does not do.","section":"trouble","order":2,"keywords":["open solver cases","units","limits","checked","not run","licence","gpl","openfoam","code_aster","openradioss","vtk","symmetry"],"headings":[{"id":"units","text":"Units","level":2},{"id":"what-was-checked","text":"What was checked","level":2},{"id":"what-was-not","text":"What was not","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"units\"\u003EUnits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft models are in millimetres. Each case says its units in every file header and in its README.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlow: metres, kilograms, seconds. Coordinates are written × 0.001. Pressure in the files is kinematic (pressure ÷ density); the forces file is in newtons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStress and frequencies: mm, tonne, s, so forces are N and stresses MPa. Density is written in t/mm³ (kg/m³ × 1e-12), gravity as 9810 mm/s². A force on faces is written as force ÷ area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHeat: mm, W, °C. Conductivity W/(m·K) × 0.001, heat flux and film coefficient × 1e-6.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop and impact: tonne, mm, s. Speeds in mm/s (m/s × 1000); the run time you type in ms is written in s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-checked\"\u003EWhat was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each kind, a strict reader written for the tests reads every written file back: brackets and keywords, every patch or group named anywhere exists everywhere it must, node and element counts, node order and orientation of every element on the geometry, the fixed column widths of the impact decks, and the unit conversions against hand calculations. The keywords were compared with the tutorial and test cases of each project as read on 11 November 2026: OpenFOAM v2412 and OpenFOAM-13, code_aster 17, and OpenRadioss input version 2022.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-not\"\u003EWhat was not\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo case from this add-on has yet been run in OpenFOAM, code_aster or OpenRadioss. A file can be well formed and still not converge, or need a setting changed for your version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe result readers were tested on small files written by hand in the documented layouts, not on real solver output. The code_aster listing layout is described by its manual as not fixed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe sign rule of an imposed heat flux in code_aster was not confirmed: check it before you rely on a flux face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhich way a tetrahedron must be ordered for OpenRadioss, and the side of its rigid floor that acts, were read from its source and guide, not seen in a run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat it does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt does not add, download or start the solvers. They are separate programs under their own licences (OpenFOAM and code_aster: GNU GPL version 3 or later; OpenRadioss: GNU AGPL version 3 or later). CadCraft only writes text files for them and reads text files they write.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlow: steady turbulent (k-omega SST with wall functions) and transient laminar only; incompressible; one fluid; no heat, no moving parts. A face picked as symmetry is written as a slip patch, which is the same thing on a flat face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStress: linear elastic, one material, fixed faces only (no sliding supports), no contact.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EImpact: one part, one material, tetrahedra only; Johnson-Cook numbers are yours to type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EResults: surfaces only (no cut plane through a flow), read from legacy ASCII VTK files and the code_aster text listing. A big surface is thinned to fit the display.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAssemblies: the part in the window only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Units PartCraft models are in millimetres. Each case says its units in every file header and in its README. Flow: metres, kilograms, seconds. Coordinates are written × 0.001. Pressure in the files is kinematic (pressure ÷ density); the forces file is in newtons. Stress and frequencies: mm, tonne, s, so forces are N and stresses MPa. Density is written in t/mm³ (kg/m³ × 1e-12), gravity as 9810 mm/s². A force on faces is written as force ÷ area. Heat: mm, W, °C. Conductivity W/(m·K) × 0.001, heat flux and film coefficient × 1e-6. Drop and impact: tonne, mm, s. Speeds in mm/s (m/s × 1000); the run time you type in ms is written in s. What was checked For each kind, a strict reader written for the tests reads every written file back: brackets and keywords, every patch or group named anywhere exists everywhere it must, node and element counts, node order and orientation of every element on the geometry, the fixed column widths of the impact decks, and the unit conversions against hand calculations. The keywords were compared with the tutorial and test cases of each project as read on 11 November 2026: OpenFOAM v2412 and OpenFOAM-13, code_aster 17, and OpenRadioss input version 2022. What was not No case from this add-on has yet been run in OpenFOAM, code_aster or OpenRadioss. A file can be well formed and still not converge, or need a setting changed for your version. The result readers were tested on small files written by hand in the documented layouts, not on real solver output. The code_aster listing layout is described by its manual as not fixed. The sign rule of an imposed heat flux in code_aster was not confirmed: check it before you rely on a flux face. Which way a tetrahedron must be ordered for OpenRadioss, and the side of its rigid floor that acts, were read from its source and guide, not seen in a run. What it does not do It does not add, download or start the solvers. They are separate programs under their own licences (OpenFOAM and code_aster: GNU GPL version 3 or later; OpenRadioss: GNU AGPL version 3 or later). CadCraft only writes text files for them and reads text files they write. Flow: steady turbulent (k-omega SST with wall functions) and transient laminar only; incompressible; one fluid; no heat, no moving parts. A face picked as symmetry is written as a slip patch, which is the same thing on a flat face. Stress: linear elastic, one material, fixed faces only (no sliding supports), no contact. Impact: one part, one material, tetrahedra only; Johnson-Cook numbers are yours to type. Results: surfaces only (no cut plane through a flow), read from legacy ASCII VTK files and the code_aster text listing. A big surface is thinned to fit the display. Assemblies: the part in the window only."}]};
